WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
In the grand scheme of things, esports is still pretty young.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.240
Despite that, we’ve seen a lot of players come and go.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.720
Some ride off with honor into the sunset.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.300
Some make tearful farewells to the bittersweet cheers of their fans.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.360
and some exit quietly, looking to get away from esports’ harsh spotlight.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.420
But some players do the opposite.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.920
Some players go out in a blaze so bright that everybody in the industry can't help but notice.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.380
One of the most notable esports exit infernos was started by former Counter Logic Gaming midlaner

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.760
Austin “Link” Shin.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.580
And the fuel for that fire? His now famous Donezo Manifesto.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.560
Link's manifesto is a relic from a time before the franchised NA LCS.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.360
For most of his career, Link, played for CLG.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.240
He was even with the team before the LCS formed.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.120
He had helped define CLG's identity over his three years with the team, but there were some struggles.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.020
Though CLG were one of the stronger teams in NA.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.320
They were never quite able to beat their demons:

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.340
Team SoloMid and Cloud9.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:11.360
CLG also had to fight their way out of relegation twice. Making them one of the most inconsistent teams

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.060
in NA, despite their top tier status.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:30.700
(Casting)

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.900
And eight months after that relegation run,

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.820
Link threw in the towel, left the team, and penned a "farewell letter."

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.440
The letter itself opened with a promise that Link wasn't just gonna straight up flame people.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.220
And it started out like any esports career chronicle.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.240
Link had a rampant passion for gaming, a tense relationship with his parents and

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.140
in the end he had to make a choice between an education, or an esports career.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:59.340
But as Link's manifesto began delving into his time on CLG. He did actually start to straight up flame people.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.400
He flamed his teammates for their not practicing and lack of independence in game,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.880
saying that "CLG shotcalling, is like babysitting."

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.840
He even flamed the game itself and how it was played.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.320
Saying that, "League of Legends is devolving into a game that I don't even recognize anymore."

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.860
But more than anything or anyone, Link flamed Doublelift and his fleeting trust in his teammates.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:33.640
Link wrote, "Once Doublelift loses respect for anyone, you're f***ing donezo."

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:40.420
The vitriolic 17-page long document skyrocketed to the League of Legends subreddit,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.880
and exploded across social media.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.700
The community dubbed it the "Donezo Manifesto,"

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.880
immediately cementing its place in League meme history.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.720
Dyrus teased his own version of the Donezo Manifesto when he retired but eventually decided against it.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.320
Alas we will never see the Dyrus Papyrus.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.020
"So f***ing funny."

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.200
And CLG memed on it too.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.740
When their former ADC sub Yoona wrote his own Donezo Manifesto, which the team submitted to the

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.760
League subreddit from their own account.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.340
But if you peel back the memes and the drama.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:21.320
The letter stands as a unique if very biased look at the NA LCS at a time when it was still growing into what it

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.300
would become today.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.700
There were probably a lot of factors that lead to Link's frustration.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:30.220
He ran away from home. He put an education at Berkeley on hold

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:35.020
and he was locked into a contract with CLG that he really didn't want to be in towards the end.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.200
But the lack of infrastructure couldn't have helped things either.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.660
CLG had chosen a coach who was working remotely from South Korea,

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:47.020
and the huge support staffs that modern franchised LCS teams have today were a pipe dream back then.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:50.440
Link's letter was supposed to be this big mic drop moment.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.260
He wanted to expose the problem with his former team and teammates. And he wanted to shine a light

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.520
on a side of League of Legends that the community had never seen before.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.300
However his bias throughout the letter was called out immediately.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.260
"Now the problem with this farewell letter, it was riddled with inconsistencies.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:13.360
He'd say something and a paragraph and a half later you'd fine him say another sentence, which didn't

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.620
fit in with what he'd just said before."

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:22.100
"It was like a soap opera plot how extreme the opinions were in this and the characterization of these people."

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.480
"Yeah I have no concern with Link telling the truth."

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.980
"But when you tell the truth from 100% your perspective, you're not taking into account how other people feel

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:30.600
about it."

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.920
"And that drastically changes the equation."

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.040
"And he knows that what he did, is gonna make people hate CLG."

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:42.760
"So when I see him be like, "Oh yeah this is a farewell letter." No it's a f**k you letter."

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.240
All that considered, one would have expected Doublelift to respond with an equal amount of venom.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.260
After all he's made a name for himself talking trash.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.240
"Everyone else is trash."

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.280
And mostly backing it up on the Rift.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:05:05.280
(Casting)

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.320
Instead, Doublelift addressed almost every point in the 17-page document.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:14.760
He rebutted a few of them, but with others, he flat out admitted he was wrong and apologized.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.000
CLG finished second during the 2015 summer regular season

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.620
just after Link left.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:32.440
(Casting)

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:37.540
And then in the playoffs they went undefeated against Team Impulse in the semis and TSM in the finals.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:53.040
(Casting)

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.760
"I mean I'm speechless, it's been years of blood sweat and tears for all of us."

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.480
"Sorry my throat's shot."

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.340
"But it's truly magical to be here, thank you."

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.680
In other words, Link left before the team took off.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.380
Link helped define the competitive landscape of the NA LCS during its formative years.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:15.100
But his letter is how most people remember him.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:20.080
Hindsight is 20/20, and Link says that he regrets how his time with CLG ended.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:29.200
But it's hard not to wonder, how Link's career would have turned out in the current NA LCS environment.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.360
"1, 2, 3, CLG!"

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:35.040
An environment that has a lot more structure and support for its players.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.640
Nowadays, Link still streams. Sometimes playing League of Legends,

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.380
somtimes playing Fortnite.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.580
But he's back on track to what he was doing before his esports career.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:56.760
Studying at Berkeley.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:03.560
Link has largely moved on from esports. But his Donezo Manifesto will stand as a historical document

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:07.700
from an NA LCS that is hopefully long since passed.

